WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.301
The measurement problem.

2
00:00:01.581 --> 00:00:06.381
Lesson 1 ended with a gap: loss curves are smooth, but nobody buys loss.

3
00:00:06.661 --> 00:00:08.645
People buy the ability to do work.

4
00:00:08.925 --> 00:00:16.136
So the second half of every big claim is a measurement of capability, and the quality of the claim is the quality of that measurement.

5
00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:22.133
Capability measurement has three recurring failure modes, and this lesson is organized around them.

6
00:00:22.413 --> 00:00:31.203
Saturation: a benchmark stops discriminating because the best models score near the top, and the remaining errors are mostly broken items.

7
00:00:31.483 --> 00:00:38.800
Contamination: the test items, or close paraphrases, were in the training data, so a high score partly measures memory.

8
00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:46.397
Validity: the benchmark measures something cleaner and narrower than the work people care about, so a high score does not transfer.

9
00:00:46.677 --> 00:00:55.680
The International AI Safety Report 2026 summarizes the result with one word you will hear often: capabilities are jagged.

10
00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:05.453
Leading systems pass graduate-level science questions and still fail at counting objects in an image or recovering from basic errors in long workflows.

11
00:01:05.733 --> 00:01:07.568
A single score hides that shape.

12
00:01:07.848 --> 00:01:13.651
Your job when reading any capability claim is to ask which slice of the jagged profile it measured.
